.TH zfile "" "Command"
.SH NAME
zfile \- GUI file manager
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBzfile [\fIoptions\fB] [\fIdirectory\fB]\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
.HS
zfile -- GUI file manager
Usage:	zfile [ options ] [ directory ]
Keys:	^P/^N select, ^Z/^V page, Return enter dir, BS parent
	e edit, c copy, m move, d delete, k mkdir, g go (also F2-F7)
.HE
.B zfile
is the file manager of the
.SM COHERENT
GUI.
It must be run under the
.B zview
desktop.
It shows one directory at a time in `ls \-l' style \- mode string,
name (directories with a trailing
.BR / ),
size and date \- with directories sorted first, then files,
alphabetically.
A button bar offers
.B "Edit, Copy, Move, Delete, Mkdir"
and
.BR Go ,
and a path line shows the current directory.
.PP
The
.I directory
argument must be an absolute path (starting with
.BR / );
without one,
.B zfile
starts at the root.
.B zfile
also accepts the standard window
.I options
of GUI applications; see
.BR zview .
.PP
Keyboard commands (the cursor keypad maps onto the control keys
as usual):
.DS
.ta 6n 22n
	\fB^P, ^N\fR	select the previous / next entry
	\fB^Z, ^B\fR	page the list up
	\fB^V, ^F, SPACE\fR	page the list down
	\fB^A, CLR/HOME\fR	select the first entry
	\fB^E\fR	select the last entry
	\fBRETURN\fR	enter the selected directory
	\fBBS\fR	go to the parent directory
	\fBHELP\fR	show the help summary
	\fBe, F2\fR	Edit: open the selected file in zedit
	\fBc, F3\fR	Copy the selected file
	\fBm, F4\fR	Move (or rename) the selected file
	\fBd, F5\fR	Delete the selected file or empty directory
	\fBk, F6\fR	Mkdir: create a directory
	\fBg, F7\fR	Go to a typed absolute path
.DE
A left click selects an entry; clicking the selected entry again
within a second enters it if it is a directory.
The scrollbar on the left edge scrolls the list.
.PP
The operations ask for names and confirmations in dialogs
(RETURN fires the default button, ESC cancels, TAB moves between
fields).
Their semantics:
.IP \fBCopy\fR
copies an ordinary file, in
.B zfile
itself; a destination that is an existing directory receives the file
under its own name, and overwriting an existing file is confirmed.
.IP \fBMove\fR
links and unlinks, so it works within one file system only and
refuses directories.
A move within the same directory is how a file is renamed.
.IP \fBDelete\fR
unlinks a file after confirmation; a directory must be empty and is
removed with
.BR rmdir .
.IP \fBMkdir\fR
runs
.B mkdir
with the typed name, relative to the current directory.
.IP \fBGo\fR
changes to a typed absolute path.
.PP
The directory is rescanned automatically every 5 seconds when it
changes on disk; the selection is kept by name.
.SH FILES
.DS
.ta 8n 32n
	/usr/hr/bin/zedit	editor run by Edit
	/bin/mkdir, /bin/rmdir	directory operations
	/usr/hr/icons/file.icn	desktop icon
.DE
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR zview ,
.BR zedit ,
.BR ls ,
.BR cp ,
.B mv
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
All diagnostics appear in dialogs, among them:
`Cannot enter that directory',
`Can only copy an ordinary file',
`Cannot move there (other file system?)',
`That directory is not empty',
`Give an absolute path (starting /)',
`Copy failed (disk full?)'.
.SH NOTES
The list holds at most 160 entries; a bigger directory is truncated
and the count reads `160+ entries'.
File names show at most 14 characters and paths may be at most 119
characters.
Dot-files are listed (only
.B .
and
.B ..
are hidden).
There is no facility for running arbitrary programs from
.BR zfile .
